<!-- ehos_app:development -->
`ehos_app`, the template the application was generated from, writes this section, and an upgrade
of the template rewrites it. Write this application's own notes below it.

## The cycle: contract first

The contracts in `lib/<%= @app %>/contracts/` are the agreement between the business and this
application: what may be done, by whom, what is recorded, and what must always hold. Pages, APIs
and jobs serve them. So a change starts in the contract, is checked there, and only then reaches
the code that implements it.

```text
1 understand  →  2 change the contract  →  3 check it  →  4 implement  →  5 gate and PR  →  6 deploy
```

A step with a skill below is done with it: invoke it through the Skill tool, never from what you
remember of it. A model invokes these, `/ehos-app:plan` beyond a small fix, and
`/ehos-app:elixir-anti-patterns-review` when a review asks for it; `/ehos-app:ship` and
`/ehos-app:review` are the user's to start. One of these your harness does not know means its
plugin did not load: tell the user, and in a run with no user, stop and report it.

| Step | When | Skill |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | a new process, from its workshop's material | `/eh-os-contract:contract-distillation` |
| 1 | a new entity or action | `/eh-os-contract:module-boundary-check` |
| 2 | a new event | `/eh-os-contract:add-event` |
| 2 | any change to a deployed contract | `/eh-os-contract:change-contract` |
| 2 | a command, event or field added or changed | `/ehos-app:sample-data` |
| 4 | a unit's decider | `/eh-os-contract:write-decider` |
| 4 | a policy, a process manager, an effect or a read model | `/eh-os-contract:write-reaction` |
| 4 | any other Elixir, and every test | `/ehos-app:elixir` |
| 5 | before the pull request | `/ehos-app:pre-pr-review` |
| 5 | to write or rewrite a pull request's description alone | `/ehos-app:pull-request` |
| 5 | after the pull request, or a workaround | `/ehos-app:retro` |

### 1. Find what the change does to the contract

- Read [VISION.md](VISION.md), then the contract the change touches.
  `mix eh_os_contract.view --details` prints the domain and every contract as a tree, and
  `mix eh_os_contract.glossary` the words they use.
- Decide whether the change belongs in a contract at all. A new business fact, rule, actor, command,
  query, publication or promise does. Distil a process that is new from its workshop's material
  first (`/eh-os-contract:contract-distillation`); for a new entity or action, decide where it lives
  and as which declaration with `/eh-os-contract:module-boundary-check`. A page's layout, a refactor
  that keeps every command's behaviour, or a performance fix does not: go to step 4.
- Name what changes: the commands and who may issue them, the events, the invariants, the read
  models and queries, what other applications read, the promises and their deadlines.

### 2. Change the contract

- Edit the domain (`lib/<%= @app %>/domain.ex`) and the contract, by the rules in
  [lib/<%= @app %>/contracts/AGENTS.md](lib/<%= @app %>/contracts/AGENTS.md).
- Give every new command a scenario that succeeds, and every new invariant a `rejected` scenario.
  A new event: `/eh-os-contract:add-event`.
- **A deployed contract has history.** Every stored event is read again with today's
  declaration. Renaming or removing an event or a field, changing a type, a unit's identity or
  the contract's `id:` breaks what is stored; adding an optional field does not. Decide this
  before editing, with `/eh-os-contract:change-contract`, and say in the pull request what the
  change does to stored events.
- **Some changes are visible outside the application**, and the person who owns the process
  agrees to them first: a command's name, fields or `by:` (it is a capability EHOS grants), a
  publication (a new shape is a new version, never a change in place), a promise or a deadline.

### 3. Check the contract before writing code

```sh
mix compile --warnings-as-errors   # the contract compiles, and the rules between modules hold
mix eh_os_contract.check           # the whole-program rules, each finding named in rules.md
mix eh_os_contract.schema --check  # every event still reads the rows eh_os_contract.lock records
mix test                           # the scenarios run against the implementation
```

A test file with `use EhOsContract.ScenarioCase, contract: <Contract>` turns every scenario into a
test. `mix eh_os_contract.check` names what the contract declares and nothing implements yet: that
is the next step's list.

### 4. Implement

- A command is decided by a module that uses the contract (`use <Contract>, aggregate: <unit>`)
  and answers with `emit/1` or `reject/1`; the engine refuses an event the command does not
  declare (`/eh-os-contract:write-decider`; for a policy, an effect or a read model,
  `/eh-os-contract:write-reaction`). `<%= @contract_dir %>/getting-started.md` shows the whole
  loop.
- Test each command the way a caller reaches it, through `/api` with a credential
  ([TESTING.md](TESTING.md)).
- Then build what serves the contract: pages, LiveViews, jobs, read models.

### 5. Gate and pull request

```sh
mix precommit     # audits the dependencies, compiles, checks the contracts, formats, runs the tests
ehosctl verify    # what CI runs; signed in to EHOS, it also has EHOS check ehos.yaml and the manifest
```

Review the change with `/ehos-app:pre-pr-review`, which records the reviewed commit
(`MIX_ENV=dev mix ehos_app.review record`, since `ehos_app` is a dependency in `:dev`): the hook in
`.claude/settings.json` refuses to open a pull request from any other. Its last step,
`/ehos-app:pull-request`, opens it with what the change does to the contract (commands, events,
capabilities, publications, stored events) and what the review found under `## Review`. CI runs
`ehosctl verify` on every pull request, and the `review` workflow checks that section. After the
pull request, `/ehos-app:retro` files what got in the way where the platform can fix it.

## Running and trying it

`mix setup` fetches the dependencies, creates and migrates the database `<%= @app %>_dev`, and
installs the asset tools; `mix phx.server` (or `iex -S mix phx.server`) serves the application on
port <%= @web_port %> and its health checks on <%= @health_port %>.

- **The ports are the ones `ehos.yaml` declares**, which EHOS runs the application on. To run two
  applications side by side, give the second other ports in `config/dev.exs` alone (`http:
  [port: …]` on the endpoint, `port:` on `<%= @web %>.Health`). EHOS run from eh-os uses 4000 too.
- **Signing in needs EHOS.** Locally there is none, so `/auth/ehos/sign-in` answers 503 and pages
  behind `:signed_in` cannot be reached.<%= if @access_page do %> `/` is the application's home page, which the template
  wrote in place of Phoenix's: its name and a link to `/access`, the page behind `:signed_in` that
  shows a signed-in person who they are here and which of the application's capabilities their
  roles grant. Both are the application's to change.<% end %> A command needs a credential EHOS issued, which the tests
  make with `EhOsContract.Auth.Testing`. To sign in, deploy `main`'s build to a local EHOS in k3d
  ([how](https://github.com/EmpoweredHouse/ehos-tools/blob/main/docs/local-ehos.md)), or see "Previews of pull requests" and "Deploying" below.
- **Claude Code's plugins** come from ehos-tools and eh-os-contract. While those repositories are
  private, Claude Code fetches them with your git credentials once the folder is trusted. Without a GitHub SSH
  key, set `CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_PREFER_HTTPS=1` and run `gh auth setup-git`.
- **Assets** are Phoenix's: `assets/` holds the application's stylesheet and script, which
  esbuild and Tailwind build from Mix.<%= cond do %><% @design_system_imported -> %> The design system comes from the `ehos_design` dependency,
  and `assets/css/app.css` only imports it ([ARCHITECTURE.md](ARCHITECTURE.md)).<% not @design_system_declined -> %> The design system comes from the `ehos_design` dependency,
  which `assets/css/app.css` does not import yet ([ARCHITECTURE.md](ARCHITECTURE.md)).<% true -> %><% end %>

## Building

On every push to `main`, CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs `ehosctl verify`, builds the release
image for amd64 and arm64, pushes it to `ghcr.io` as one index named by its digest, and publishes
the artifact `ehos-deployment`: `image`, the image's name with its digest, and `ehos.bundle/`, the
bundle of the same commit: `ehos.manifest.json`, the manifest; `graph/`, its graph documents, its
domain's, which EHOS draws in the environment's landscape of applications, and each contract's and
process module's, which the console shows inside the application; and `bundle.json`, which names
each by its SHA-256. Once the repository names its EHOS installation and EHOS trusts it on `main`
(below), CI also registers the image and the bundle there as a **build**, with the commit it came
from, its title, author and the commits before it, so EHOS knows each version before anyone deploys
it (eh-os D-146). A pull request of this repository is registered once the installation is named;
a fork's builds the image and publishes nothing. CI deploys no build: a person deploys the one they
want to try ("Deploying", below), unless the repository turns on previews of pull requests (below).

A team whose flow builds another branch than `main` changes the workflow's `push` branches and the
refs the trust accepts (below) alike; nothing else names `main`.

CI reads the private `eh_os_contract`<%= if @design_system_dep do %> and `ehos_design`<% end %>, and installs `ehosctl` from ehos-tools,
with tokens of the GitHub App *ehos-tools CI*, installed on eh-os-contract and ehos-tools: the
repository needs the secrets `EHOS_CI_APP_ID` and `EHOS_CI_APP_PRIVATE_KEY`, and its first run says
so when they are missing. To build the image yourself, give Docker a
GitHub token as a build secret, which reaches no layer of the image:

```sh
docker build --secret id=github_token,env=GITHUB_TOKEN .
```

`GITHUB_TOKEN` is best a fine-grained token that reads eh-os-contract<%= if @design_system_dep do %> and ehos-tools<% end %> alone, since
every dependency's `mix.exs` runs where it is. Set it from where you keep it, a password manager's
command line for one, rather than typing it, which leaves it in the shell's history.

## Registering builds with EHOS

CI registers each build with the installation that the repository variable `EHOSCTL_URL` names,
with no key to EHOS: `publish` exchanges its own GitHub Actions token, for whoever pushed (eh-os
D-110). Without the variable, `publish` registers nothing and its summary says how to set it.

Once per repository and installation, by someone who holds `ehos.application.register` on the
whole platform, the administrator most often:

```sh
ehosctl login https://<installation>        # once per installation; opens the browser
run=$(gh run list -w ci.yml -e push -b main -s success -L 1 --json databaseId -q '.[0].databaseId')
rm -rf _build/deploy
gh run download "$run" -n ehos-deployment -D _build/deploy
ehosctl apps register _build/deploy/ehos.bundle/ehos.manifest.json
ehosctl trust add --app <%= @ehos_name %> --repo <owner>/<repository> --ref 'refs/pull/*/merge'
gh variable set EHOSCTL_URL --body https://<installation>
gh variable set EHOSCTL_CA_PEM < <the installation's ca.crt>   # only if it has its own CA
```

Only CI's run of a push is read: a fork's pull request from a branch it calls `main` runs on `main`
too. `apps register` keeps its manifest as the application's first version; until then `trust add`
is refused. `trust add` needs `ehos.role_binding.manage` and `ehos.application.deploy` on the
application. The variables come last.

That trust registers pull requests' builds. `main`'s run publishes `ehos-deployment` without one,
and its summary says how to add `refs/heads/main`, keeping all the trust has. A run on that ref
reaches every preview of the application (eh-os D-121), and the trust cannot require the ref to be
protected, since `--protected` holds for each of its refs and GitHub protects no pull request's: so
first protect `main` with a ruleset that lets nothing onto it but a merged pull request and that no
one may bypass. EHOS's warning, when the ref is added, is answered by that ruleset.

Once per developer: they need `ehos.application.deploy` on the application, or the job's token is
refused (`actor_not_permitted`). Their first build is refused `actor_unknown` until they claim their
GitHub account from that run with `ehosctl github claim`, and another people manager confirms it
(`ehosctl people github confirm`, or the console's **GitHub accounts**) after asking them; an
installation's only people manager confirms their own.

## Previews of pull requests

With previews on, CI deploys each pull request's build, as a candidate, to an ephemeral environment
of its own, `<%= @ehos_name %>-pr-<number>`. `gh variable set EHOS_PREVIEWS --body true` turns them on, and
`gh variable delete EHOS_PREVIEWS` off, `preview-remove` with them: open pull requests' previews
then expire by themselves. The variable changes nothing in EHOS: its trust and its count of
ephemeral environments apply either way. Each open pull request holds one of the application's
ephemeral environments, three by default; a merged one holds it until it expires, 8 hours after
the last push, since its closing run gets no credential to remove it (eh-os#672). In each new
environment, a person binds the application's roles and gives the people agreement again
(eh-os#1048). Each developer also needs `ehos.application.register` on the application: the first
deployment in each new environment registers the application there.

Each push deploys again and extends the environment by 8 hours. A comment on the pull request links
to it, and says what became of it at the close. At the count, the job passes with a warning, and the
comment says how to deploy the build from the console. A person who closes a pull request without a
merge removes its preview, when EHOS lets them; a bot's close leaves it to expire. A fork's or a
bot's pull request gets no preview. The name is at most 54 characters, and with a database counts
towards the 51 EHOS allows an application's and an environment's names together in a schema's name.

### Data to try a preview on

EHOS runs the seed `ehos.yaml` names in an ephemeral environment only, never in production: after
the migration, at every start, as the schema's owner. Here it is `<%= inspect(@seed) %>.run/0`, which,
as the template writes it, runs `priv/repo/seeds.exs` with the repo and an Oban that runs no queue;
`mix ecto.setup` runs the script too. Its `add/2` records the sample facts `EHOS_SEED_PROFILE`
chooses through the contracts' commands, as `:sample_data`, each once, never in a production
environment: `/ehos-app:sample-data` writes its steps, and moves a seed an earlier version wrote.
A row the application owns the script adds once (an upsert), never into the engine's tables. The
seed ships in every image, so it holds no real data, credentials or secrets.

## Deploying

A deployment puts an image, by its digest, and its manifest in force in one environment of an
installation of EHOS. EHOS creates the application's database schema and roles, runs the
migrations, then starts the new release. The application's name in EHOS is its domain's `name:`,
`<%= @ehos_name %>` as the template wrote it.

Production is reached only by **promotion** (eh-os D-105): a version is first deployed as a
**candidate** to an ephemeral environment of the application, where it is tried, and then the
application's process owner approves its promotion, as a self-approval if they deployed it or
asked, unless four eyes is on (eh-os D-128): an administrator's `ehosctl installation` says.
To try a pull request, deploy its build from the application's **Builds** in EHOS's console, or
with `--build latest --ref refs/pull/<number>/merge` below. A preview (above) is such a candidate
too. By hand, from a run of CI on `main`:

```sh
ehosctl login https://<installation>        # once per installation; opens the browser
rm -rf _build/deploy
gh run download <run> -n ehos-deployment -D _build/deploy
ehosctl apps register _build/deploy/ehos.bundle/ehos.manifest.json   # the first time
ehosctl env create <%= @ehos_name %>-try --app <%= @ehos_name %>   # once, while it lives
ehosctl deploy <%= @ehos_name %> --env <%= @ehos_name %>-try --manifest _build/deploy/ehos.bundle/ehos.manifest.json --preview
ehosctl deploy <%= @ehos_name %> --env <%= @ehos_name %>-try --build latest --ref refs/heads/main
```

The download goes under `_build/`, which git ignores, and `gh run download` refuses to write over
an earlier one, hence the `rm`. `--preview` shows what the manifest would change, and deploys
nothing. `--build latest --ref refs/heads/main` deploys the newest build CI registered from `main`:
its image, its manifest and its graph documents together, named by its commit. `ehosctl builds
list <%= @ehos_name %> --ref refs/heads/main` lists them, and `--build <id>` deploys another. EHOS
takes a build from 0.22.0, and has `main`'s once the trust accepts it (above). Otherwise deploy the
download's files: `--image "$(cat _build/deploy/image)" --bundle _build/deploy/ehos.bundle`.
`apps register` keeps the manifest as the application's first version, which EHOS needs before it
makes the application an environment. `env create` makes the environment from the installation's
default template, once: a later version is deployed to the same one while it lives, and replaces
the candidate there, withdrawing any promotion asked for it. It expires in a day unless
`ehosctl env extend <%= @ehos_name %>-try --ttl 3d` gives it longer, from now, up to the
installation's maximum, and an application has three by default, any previews of pull requests
among them. The first deployment to a new environment also needs `ehos.application.register`.

Then, in EHOS's console, the application's **Candidates and promotions** lists the candidate:
**Promote to production**. The process owner, someone the administrator bound `ehos.process_owner`
on the application, finds it under **Approvals** (**Promotions** before EHOS 0.24.0; from 0.24.0
they are mailed a link if they have signed in before, did not ask for the promotion and, with four
eyes on, did not deploy it), reviews what it binds to, and approves it. A first promotion, or one
that changes roles or capabilities, puts them in force in production, so its approver also holds
`ehos.application.register` there ([eh-os D-119](https://github.com/EmpoweredHouse/eh-os/blob/main/docs/adr/0119-approving-a-promotion-that-puts-another-authority-in-force-needs-the-right-to-register-it-and-it-waits-only-for-whoever-holds-both.md)).
So before it, the administrator binds the process owner `ehos.registrar` in production too, a role
EHOS has from 0.11.0 that holds that capability alone, as "Who deploys, and joining the team" says:
`ehosctl role-bindings bind ehos.registrar --to <email> --app <%= @ehos_name %> --env production --installation <installation>`.
Without it **Approvals** does not list the promotion for them, though the application's own
promotions page still shows it and its review says what is missing. Keep the environment alive until
the promotion is approved: a candidate whose environment expires is no longer one, and its promotion
is withdrawn. Production's deployment is decided from the same image and manifest, against
production's own data and secrets. `ehosctl promote` and `approve` come with
[EmpoweredHouse/ehos-tools#246](https://github.com/EmpoweredHouse/ehos-tools/issues/246);
`ehosctl deploy --env production` is refused, `promotion_required`.

- **`--preview`** deploys nothing, and takes the manifest alone: EHOS checks the image when it
  is deployed. It shows what the deployment would change, the capabilities and roles it adds or
  removes among them, and every reason EHOS would refuse it.
- **`deploy`** waits until the deployment is active, or has failed, for up to ten minutes.
  `ehosctl apps show <%= @ehos_name %>` shows each environment's version and state afterwards.
- **Nobody can use it until a role is bound to them**, the administrator included:
  `ehosctl role-bindings bind <%= @ehos_name %>.<actor> --to <email> --app <%= @ehos_name %> --env production`.
  A role is the domain's name and an actor of the domain.
- **Try a command** as yourself: `ehosctl call <%= @ehos_name %> <%= @ehos_name %>.<command> field=value`.
  `ehosctl credential <%= @ehos_name %>` prints a credential for `curl`.
- **Migrations run before the new release starts**, while the old one still serves. A column or a
  table is dropped in two deploys: one that stops using it, then one that drops it.
- **Read what it logs** with `ehosctl logs <%= @ehos_name %> --env production`, or on the
  application's page in the console; `--follow` prints new lines as they come, and `--grep`,
  `--since` and `--deployment` narrow them. In production each line is a JSON object with its
  level, `request_id`, `trace.id` and `correlation_id` as fields. EHOS keeps the lines, and
  whoever may read the application's logs reads them: keep secrets and people's data out of what
  the application logs. A crashed process's report shows only the shape of its state and last
  message, and a stack frame's arity in place of its arguments, there and in the error a request
  ends in (`LogRedaction`, started first in `start/2`). An exception's own message is kept, and
  may quote a value: a `MatchError` quotes what did not match, a changeset included. The formatter
  only replaces the value of a field named exactly `password`, `secret`, `token`, `authorization`
  or `cookie` with `"[REDACTED]"`.

`ehosctl help <command>` explains each command, and the
[`ehosctl` README](https://github.com/EmpoweredHouse/ehos-tools/blob/main/packages/ehosctl/README.md)
all of them, environments and people included.

## Who deploys, and joining the team

An installation of EHOS is one company's: its own server, console, people and record. Two kinds of
people work on an application there, and they hold different things:

| | The operator | A developer who deploys |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Does | makes, upgrades and removes the server and EHOS on it; invites people | changes the application, merges to `main`, deploys what CI built, lets people in to it |
| Holds | the cloud account's key, the server's SSH key, the installation's state passphrase, the registry token, the kubeconfig | a GitHub account, an EHOS account with `ehos.application_administrator` for `<%= @ehos_name %>`, and the installation's CA certificate |

Deploying needs no cloud account, SSH key or Kubernetes: `ehosctl deploy` asks EHOS, which decides,
records it, and deploys through its own operator. The operator's keys stay with the operator.

**To let a developer in, the operator**, once `<%= @ehos_name %>` has been promoted to
`production`:

1. gives them write access to this repository, and read access to the private repositories its
   dependencies come from;
2. invites them to the installation, `ehosctl server invite <email> --installation <installation>`,
   which prints a one-time sign-in link, valid for 15 minutes: it is a password, sent to them
   alone, in a direct message to an account the operator knows is theirs. If anyone else may have
   seen it, the operator disables the person, enables them again and invites them anew;
3. binds them what deploying this application takes, and a role of the application to use it:

   ```sh
   ehosctl role-bindings bind ehos.application_administrator --to <email> --app <%= @ehos_name %> --installation <installation>
   ehosctl role-bindings bind <%= @ehos_name %>.<actor> --to <email> --app <%= @ehos_name %> --env production --installation <installation>
   ```

   `ehos.application_administrator` for `<%= @ehos_name %>` registers and deploys it, which makes
   its database schema and runs its migrations, reads its record, and binds its roles to anyone
   already invited, itself included, in any of its environments, and makes, extends and removes
   its ephemeral environments. It acts on no other application, makes no production environment,
   approves no promotion, binds no `ehos.process_owner`, and binds or classifies no data resource;
   it does see the installation's environments and resources, read-only. The administrator binds
   the application's process owner, who approves its promotions, `ehos.process_owner`, and
   `ehos.registrar` as well in production, which its first promotion and any that changes its
   roles or capabilities need. With `ehos.process_owner`, `ehos.registrar` lets them approve such
   a promotion; it registers no new version, binds no one, and puts no version in force by hand,
   since only a deployment does:

   ```sh
   ehosctl role-bindings bind ehos.process_owner --to <email> --app <%= @ehos_name %> --installation <installation>
   ehosctl role-bindings bind ehos.registrar --to <email> --app <%= @ehos_name %> --env production --installation <installation>
   ```

4. sends them the installation's CA certificate, `~/.ehos/<installation>/ca.crt` on the
   operator's machine, and its fingerprint by another channel:
   `openssl x509 -noout -fingerprint -sha256 -in ca.crt`.

**The developer** then follows the
[golden path](https://github.com/EmpoweredHouse/ehos-tools/blob/main/docs/golden-path.md) from
installing `ehosctl`: lets git read the private dependencies (`gh auth setup-git`), runs
`mix setup` and `mix precommit`, opens the link and sets a password, checks the CA's fingerprint,
signs in with `EHOSCTL_CA_FILE=<ca.crt> ehosctl login https://<the console>`, and deploys as above.

**When someone leaves**, the operator runs `ehosctl people disable <email> --installation
<installation>`, which ends their sign-in on the installation, and removes their roles too, since
they would hold again were the person enabled (`ehosctl role-bindings list`, then `unbind`); their
GitHub access is removed there.

## Keeping it current

`ehosctl versions` shows, for `ehosctl`, EHOS, `ehos_app`, `eh_os_contract` and `ehos_design`, the
version in use, the newest release, whether the installation accepts it, and the command that
moves to it. `ehosctl changelog <component>` prints what changed since the version in use.

A later `ehos_app` is taken by pointing its dependency in `mix.exs` at the release: its `ref:` at
the commit the release's tag names, and its requirement at the version (`"== X.Y.Z"`), as
`ehosctl versions` prints them; then `mix deps.get` and `mix ehos_app.install`. The installer rewrites the sections
it owns, like this one, moves `eh_os_contract` and both plugins with it, and leaves
everything else in the project as it is. `mix precommit` then shows what a newer engine asks of the
contracts.

A package in `mix.lock` that is retired or has a security advisory fails `mix precommit` and CI
until it is updated or acknowledged ([TESTING.md](TESTING.md), "Dependency advisories").
<!-- /ehos_app:development -->
